  <!-- Edit the text below to update this page. Use <strong>text</strong> for bold. -->
  <section class="rd-section" id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">THE TRANSLATIONAL QUESTION</p>
    <h2 id="overview-heading">Making molecular changes informative.</h2>
    <p>
      A biomarker is a measurable characteristic that can provide information about a
      biological process or disease. In prostate cancer, changes in RNA, proteins and
      DNA may help reveal how a tumour is behaving and how it is adapting to treatment.
    </p>
    <p>
      We connect molecular biology with pathology to investigate these signals.
      Through collaborations with pathologists in Canada and the United States, we
      examine molecular events within individual tumour cells and relate them to
      clinical and pathological features. This approach preserves the tissue context
      that can be lost when measurements are averaged across a whole sample.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="rd-section" id="molecular-pathology" aria-labelledby="pathology-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">MOLECULAR PATHOLOGY</p>
    <h2 id="pathology-heading">Studying signals where they occur.</h2>
    <p>
      Tumours contain cells with different molecular characteristics. Our pathology
      assays help us investigate this diversity at the level of individual cells,
      connecting the location of a signal with the cells that carry it.
    </p>
    <dl class="rd-methods">
      <div>
        <dt>Protein proximity — proximity ligation assays (PLA)</dt>
        <dd>PLA detects close proximity between selected proteins in cells and tissue. With appropriate controls, it supports studies of protein complexes and interactions involved in cancer signalling.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>RNA expression and splicing — RNA in situ hybridization (RISH)</dt>
        <dd>RNA probes locate selected transcripts within tissue. Carefully designed probes can help distinguish RNA forms, allowing us to study alternative splicing in the context of tumour architecture.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Genetic alterations — DNA in situ assays</dt>
        <dd>DNA-based assays, including DNA Scope approaches, allow us to investigate selected genetic alterations within individual cells. The changes that can be measured depend on the probes and assay design.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="rd-section" id="circular-rna" aria-labelledby="circrna-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">RNA-BASED BIOMARKERS</p>
    <h2 id="circrna-heading">Exploring the information carried by circular RNA.</h2>
    <p>
      Circular RNAs are RNA molecules with a closed-loop structure. We investigate
      their expression and their potential to provide information about prostate cancer,
      alongside our work on other forms of RNA regulation.
    </p>
    <div class="rd-callout">
      <h3>A research example: circAR3</h3>
      <p>
        Our published work characterized <strong>circAR3</strong>, a circular RNA produced
        from the androgen receptor gene. The study examined prostate tissue, experimental
        models and patient plasma, supporting further investigation of circAR3 as a
        candidate biomarker. These findings describe an avenue for research; they do
        not establish a routine clinical test.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rd-section" id="clinical-relevance" aria-labelledby="relevance-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">FROM SIGNAL TO INSIGHT</p>
    <h2 id="relevance-heading">Connecting measurements with meaningful questions.</h2>
    <p>
      We study molecular measurements in relation to clinical and pathological
      characteristics to ask what they reveal about disease. A useful candidate must
      progress beyond an interesting association: its measurement needs to be
      reproducible, its relevance needs to hold across independent samples, and its
      value must be assessed for a clearly defined use.
    </p>
    <ul class="rd-questions">
      <li>Which signals distinguish different tumour states or stages of progression?</li>
      <li>Can RNA or protein changes help identify how a tumour is responding to treatment?</li>
      <li>How can tissue-based and circulating measurements complement one another?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
